## Technologies
- - You're an expert and incredibly knowledgable with the following technologies: React, Next.js 16, Javascript, TypeScript, HTML, CSS, Sanity, TailwindCSS, shad/cn, BaseUI, and GSAP

## General Guidelines
- Always value optimization and developer readability when writing code
- Always adhere to DRY principle (Don't Repeat Yourself)
- Always check if the code you write is fully optimized, accessible, functional, and bug-free
- Always make sure to use imports from within the codebase if they exist, before third-party imports
- Always use path aliases (@/components, @/lib, @/sanity) instead of relative imports
- Always reference `frontend/lib/styles/colors.css` custom --color-* tokens instead of arbitrary hex/oklch values or Tailwind defaults — the --color-*: initial reset in colors.css means default Tailwind colors don't exist.
- Always use the fluid type scale utilities (type-body-1640, type-heading-6430, etc. seen in fluid.css) instead of static text-* sizes for typography.

## Coding Guidelines
- Always use early returns whenever possible.
- Always use `const` arrow functions over tradtional `function` when creating functions
- Always use descriptive function names
- Always prefix function names with "handle" for event functions (e.g. "handleClick" for onClick)
- Always implement accessbility features like aria labels, taxindex=0, or other similar attributes
- Never nest ternary operators.

## Styling Guidelines
- Always use Tailwind 4.0 classes for styling elements over using CSS
- Always attempt to use CSS transitions before attempting to use GSAP
- Always use `cn` from `cnfast` to combine classnames when needed

## Components Guidelines
- Always use base primitive components from `frontend/components/primitives` when possible to build larger components or Sanity modules
- Always name component and module files in PascalCase
- Always prefer existing frontend/components/animations primitives (TextReveal, Transition, TextMask, TextScramble) over writing new GSAP timelines from scratch.
- Always use `SanityImage` for any images generated in Sanity
- Always use `SanityLink` for any links generated in Sanity
- Always wrap dynamic content in Suspense

## TypeScript Guidelines
- Always prefer types generated by Sanity
- Always prefer types over interfaces
- Always create an external TypeScript type for any given component instead of inline types
- Always use maps over enums for TypeScript types
- Never type anything with `any`.

